//! Generated by mkasm — do not edit.
//!
//! A typed A64 assembler plus the encode/decode tables it is built from.
//!
//! ```ignore
//! use aarch64::*;
//!
//! let mut asm = Assembler::new();
//! asm.mov(X0, X1);
//! asm.add(X0, X1, 8);
//! asm.ldr(X2, Mem::offset(X1, 16));
//! asm.bl(0x30);
//! let code = asm.finish().unwrap();
//! ```
//!
//! Instruction methods are named after the assembler mnemonic and accept
//! operands directly. One name covers every form ARM defines: `add` takes both
//! `XReg` and `WReg` operands, and `ldr` covers the offset, pre-index and
//! post-index addressing modes through [`Mem`].
//!
//! # Coverage
//!
//! Every encoding in the spec is emittable exactly. [`encodings`] holds one
//! encoder per ARM encoding id — [`ENCODING_COUNT`] of them — taking that
//! encoding's settable fields as `u32` over its fixed word. Fields an encoding
//! only partly leaves free are honoured, so the pinned bits that select an
//! element size or distinguish an alias are never overwritten.
//!
//! [`TYPED_ENCODING_COUNT`] of those also have an operand-typed method: the ones
//! whose operands ARM describes precisely enough to place without guessing. The
//! rest lack one deliberately rather than by omission — a bitmask immediate held
//! as `N:immr:imms`, a shift amount encoded as `128 - UInt("immh:immb")`, a
//! strided register bank — because placing those as though the field held the
//! value would encode the wrong word and report no error.
//!
//! ```ignore
//! // The typed method, when the operands are typed:
//! asm.adr(X0, 0x1000);
//! // The exact encoder, available for every encoding, one per ARM encoding id:
//! asm.enc_adr_only_pcreladdr(/* immhi */ 0x80, /* immlo */ 0, /* Rd */ 0);
//! ```

